    {
      "id": "quadratic-functions",
      "title": "Quadratic Functions & Transformations",
      "estimatedHours": 6,
      "outcomes": [
        {
          "code": "RF1",
          "description": "- It is expected that students will: 1. Factor polynomial expressions of the form: ax2 + bx + c, a ≠ 0\n- A2x2 – b2y2, a ≠ 0, b ≠ 0\n- A(f(x))2 + b(f(x)) + c, a ≠ 0\n- A2(f(x))2 – b2(g(y))2, a ≠ 0, b ≠ 0\n- Where a, b and c are rational numbers. Notes",
          "strand": "Relations and Functions"
        },
        {
          "code": "RF3",
          "description": "- It is expected that students will: 3. Analyze quadratic functions of the form y = a(x – p)2 + q and determine the\n- Vertex\n- Domain and range\n- Direction of opening\n- Axis of symmetry",
          "strand": "Relations and Functions"
        },
        {
          "code": "RF4",
          "description": "- It is expected that students will: 4. Analyze quadratic functions of the form y = ax2 + bx + c to identify characteristics of the corresponding graph, including\n- Vertex\n- Domain and range\n- Direction of opening\n- Axis of symmetry",
          "strand": "Relations and Functions"
        },
        {
          "code": "RF5",
          "description": "- Solve problems that involve quadratic equations. [ICT: C6–4.1]",
          "strand": "Relations and Functions"
        },
        {
          "code": "RF11",
          "description": "- Graph and analyze the reciprocals of linear and quadratic functions. [ICT: C6–4.1, C6–4.3]\n- Compare the graphs of y = f(x) and y = 1/f(x).\n- Identify x-values where y = 1/f(x) has vertical asymptotes and relate them to the non-permissible values of the related rational expression.\n- Graph y = 1/f(x) from a given linear or quadratic y = f(x), and graph y = f(x) from a given reciprocal graph; explain the strategies used.",
          "type": "general"
        },
        {
          "code": "RF2",
          "description": "- Graph and analyze absolute value functions (limited to linear and quadratic functions) to solve problems. [ICT: C6–4.1, C6–4.3]",
          "type": "general"
        },
        {
          "code": "RF6",
          "description": "- Model situations using systems of linear–quadratic or quadratic–quadratic equations in two variables.\n- Relate a given system to its problem context.\n- Determine and verify solutions for such systems algebraically and graphically (with technology). [ICT: C6–4.1, C6–4.4]\n- Explain the meaning of points of intersection and why such systems may have zero, one, two, or infinitely many solutions.\n- Solve problems involving these systems and justify the strategy used.",
          "type": "general"
        },
        {
          "code": "RF7",
          "description": "- Solve problems that involve linear and quadratic inequalities in two variables, excluding systems of linear inequalities. [ICT: C6–4.1, C6–4.3]\n- Use test points to determine the solution region of an inequality.\n- Explain when to use a solid versus a broken boundary line.\n- Sketch the graph of a linear or quadratic inequality, with or without technology.\n- Create an inequality to model a given problem, and solve and interpret the result.",
          "type": "general"
        },
        {
          "code": "RF8",
          "description": "- Solve problems that involve quadratic inequalities in one variable.\n- 1 Determine the solution of a quadratic inequality in one variable using strategies such as sign analysis, case analysis, graphing, or roots and test points, and explain the strategy used.\n- 2 Represent and solve a problem that involves a quadratic inequality in one variable.\n- 3 Interpret the solution to a problem that involves a quadratic inequality in one variable.",
          "type": "general"
        }
      ],
      "topics": [
        "Quadratic functions in standard, vertex, and factored form",
        "Transformations of quadratic functions",
        "Graphing parabolas",
        "Completing the square",
        "Solving quadratic equations",
        "The quadratic formula",
        "Applications of quadratic functions"
      ],
